WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:22.520
This week on the Computer Chronicles, the best software of the year according to the

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.920
Software Publishers Association.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.880
We'll feature seven new computer programs that won Codys at this year's SPA Excellence

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.560
in Software Awards.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.200
We'll show you the Thinking Things collection from Edmark, a program called Hollywood from

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.120
Theatrics, Dark Forces from LucasArts, and Rick Smullin's Passage to Vietnam.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.800
All this plus Giles Online, this week's computer news and my pick of the week, coming up next

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.040
on the Computer Chronicles.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.080
The Computer Chronicles is made possible in part by Acer America, proud supporters of

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:06.200
intelligent programming, computer or otherwise.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:17.800
And by Lycos, a comprehensive index of the internet, www.lycos.com.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.520
Hi and welcome to the Computer Chronicles.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.320
I'm Stuart Shafae.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.960
This is a Cody.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.680
It is to software developers what the Oscar is to movie producers.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.100
It means you've been recognized by your peers for producing one of the best computer programs

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.180
of the year.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.520
The Cody for best educational software program this year went to Thinking Things Collection

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:41.780
3 from Edmark, a program designed to teach children thinking and problem-solving skills.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.080
And this is Renee and Terry who use it in school.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.800
And Terry, tell me how do you use this software in your class?

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.400
Well, you can use this software for teaching problem-solving and deductive reasoning.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.760
You can use it to teach the basic principles of physics.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.760
It's a real teaching tool.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:57.760
I mean, you really use it to teach.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:01:58.760
It's not just a game.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.760
It's very flexible.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.960
You can customize it to...

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:02.960
Okay.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.160
So could you and Renee show us some of the things in the program?

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.520
Sure can.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.440
Take you first to the Fripple House.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.440
The Fripple House.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.440
Fripple Shop, I guess.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:11.440
Fripple Place.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.440
Fripple Place, yeah.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.440
And...

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.440
And what do we learn with the Fripple Place?

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:20.400
In the Fripple Place at the beginning level, kids are learning to build their reading comprehension

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.760
as they read the clues in each room and try to put the Fripples where they belong in the

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:26.760
house.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:27.760
Okay.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.080
So let's try to get the Fripple House up here.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.880
Place at least one Fripple in every room.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:32.880
Okay.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.880
So Renee, why don't you tell us what you're doing there?

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:35.880
Okay.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:42.920
I'm going to place this Fripple here because it's the only Fripple that's red with stripes.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:50.720
And I'm going to place this Fripple there because he's the only Fripple with a cowboy

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.720
hat.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.720
All right.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.840
So you have to know how to read the words and also apply the logic to the different

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.520
little Fripples there, right?

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.520
Yes.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.520
And this is the easy level.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.040
There's a growth slide that allows teachers and parents to adjust the level and individualize

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.040
the program for each student.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.040
Okay.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.040
So we're going to advanced.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.240
We're going to a more advanced level.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.760
And in the more advanced level, kids are really using deductive reasoning, and they have to

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:18.000
analyze and synthesize information in order to form conclusions.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.920
This is really more like a puzzle, I guess, isn't it, Renee?

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:21.920
Yes.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.880
So I mean, now the little Fripples actually tell you what their conditions are if you

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.520
click on them?

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.240
You just have to put your mouse on them.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.240
Okay.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.200
So the Fripples above and below me have straight hair, so you have to make sure he's in a place

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.960
where the guy below has straight hair and the guy above has straight hair.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.520
So this is hard.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.520
Yes.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.440
And it gets you thinking about how the Fripples are related, because that's really the key

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.520
to solving the problem.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.520
Now you said there's a physics part of this, too?

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:49.520
Yeah.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.520
Let's go to the carving blocks activity.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.520
Good boy.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.760
In this activity, the kids have a virtual workspace where they can actually experiment

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:02.160
with the basic principles of physics, with gravity, friction, motion, inertia.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.160
And they're given a work surface that is actually on the horizontal plane rather than the vertical

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.160
plane.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:12.920
And they have tools that they use to carve trenches of varying widths and depths.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.440
They can drill holes in the surface.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:15.440
Okay.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.520
So right now, Renee, you just sort of made that little rut in there, huh?

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.520
And what are you actually going to do?

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.520
You're going to, like, roll balls in this or something?

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.520
Yeah.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:31.400
You can make ramps, and then you're given a set of balls, and you can roll them down.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.520
And then there's a server you can bring out.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.720
It actually brings the balls out?

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:36.720
No.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.000
You go like this, and it comes out.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.000
Okay.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.080
And real quick, we have a little bit of time, Renee.

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.760
You can change the physical characteristics here, like the friction and the gravity and

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:48.760
stuff like that.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:49.760
Yeah.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:50.760
Show me how.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:51.760
You can change the gravity.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:53.600
So, like, gravity on a different planet?

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.600
Yeah.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.800
And then you can change the friction.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.560
And you could pour oil on there and stuff like that?

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:01.560
Yes.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.840
You can also tilt the surface in any direction.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.840
Oh, okay.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.440
So we have a lot of built-in ideas here that kind of illustrate these principles of physics

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.360
and get you started that we can go to.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:17.280
Why don't we go to the idea, the first idea?

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.960
The friction is strong, and the gravity is light.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.440
But because we have applied oil onto this side, the balls will keep rolling.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:28.560
So you can really learn a lot about physics here, huh?

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.560
Yeah.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.560
It's a very cool program.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:31.560
Thank you both very much.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.720
On today's program, we'll only have time to look at seven of the Cody Award winners, but

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:38.920
overall the SBA gave away 35 Codys.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:56.960
It all took place at a gala event held right here in San Francisco.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:02.560
Tonight is the culmination of our awards ceremony as we pick the best of this year's software.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:08.200
There were over 900 titles nominated this year, representing the work of several hundred

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.200
companies.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.280
San Francisco was host to the 11th Annual Excellence in Software Awards, and it was

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:20.640
clear almost from the outset that the internet and home computing had gained a new importance.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:27.360
The consumer is the king of new computer software and hardware, and for years the industry was

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.640
driven by large corporate customers.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.480
But now the big growth curve is in the home.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.100
Intuit won awards for Best Consumer Upgrade and Home Productivity Product for Quicken

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.720
Deluxe and Quicken Financial Suite.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.240
Netscape Navigator was named Best Consumer Software, and Knowledge Adventure received

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.040
the Cody for Best Home Learning Program for Children with Jumpstart First Grade.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:55.080
Nile Passage to Egypt by the Discovery Channel Multimedia won the award for Best Adult Home

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.080
Learning Program.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:02.360
Mech was named Best Adventure Game for Oregon Trail 2, and the Best Strategy Program award

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.020
was given to Ascendancy by The Logic Factory.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:10.400
The Internet Awards spanned across several categories, but one company managed almost

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:13.680
a clean sweep in one area.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:21.280
And the Cody Award goes to Netscape Navigator, Personal Edition, Netscape Corporation.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.120
The Best Programming Tool was QuickTime VR from Apple.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.360
Best Software for People with Special Needs went to Curriculum Corporation for Choosing

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.800
Success.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.200
IndyCar Racing 2 from Papyrus was recognized as the Best Simulation Program.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:40.800
The Best Debut of the Year award was given to Storm Software for EasyPhoto.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.640
And Best New Use of the Computer, Netscape Navigator.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:08:02.360
For the Computer Chronicles, I'm Giles Bateman.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:07.600
In the category of Best Home Creativity Software Program, the Cody was awarded to Theatrix

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:11.760
Interactive for their very clever new program called Simply Hollywood.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.600
And I guess, Bob, what you do with Hollywood is you're a Hollywood producer, a director,

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.040
and you can make your own movies?

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:17.040
Exactly.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.600
It really is a movie production tool for kids of all ages that they can use to animate,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.200
write dialogue, and play back everything sequenced the way that they chose to do it.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.140
And it's pretty darn sophisticated, actually, for a little home program.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.600
Show us how it's like to make a movie.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:31.600
Sure.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:34.200
I've got a choice of a variety of different backgrounds.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.400
I can pick something that's in the jungle, or I can go to something that is a car.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:39.400
OK.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.000
So I can pick my opening set.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.000
Let's select that.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:49.640
And we'll put in a title down here at the bottom that says, Stuart in the car.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:50.920
And now we want to pick Stuart.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.720
So maybe we'll make you Paul Mann.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.400
And I want to define your character.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:01.000
So I've got to change you from the default of Chuck to Stuart.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.480
So again, I can pick from a library of existing cartoon characters, but then give this character

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.480
characteristics.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.480
You want to give it a personality.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.560
So it helps the child think about character development.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.600
And we have a reporter here.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.400
And let's say that you're maybe into, you look like a sunbather.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:17.400
OK.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.400
And we can check to see if we...

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.400
My name is Stuart.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.560
I am a reporter, and I am interested in sunbathing.

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.160
So we can check that voice.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.560
And there are 26 different voices to choose from.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:29.560
You said children, by the way.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.640
I mean, I would think adults would play with this too.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:37.000
We get a lot of interest from adults who have used this for just having fun, to parties,

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:38.000
to presentations.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.000
All right.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.000
So we pick a character.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.000
Now, we can pick more than one character, I take it, so we can have dialogue?

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.000
Sure.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.840
But let's move you over here for a second.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:49.080
We'll get into the script mode that brings up the script.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.360
And it knows already that it's you.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.720
We've changed your name, that you're in the car.

00:09:53.720 --> 00:10:02.680
And we can say, well, I'm waiting for my girlfriend.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:04.760
So you're literally writing the script.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:05.760
Right.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.600
And then you've got a text-to-speech converter, so the character will actually read those

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:09.600
words.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:10.600
Exactly.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:14.000
Now, I can go in and add a variety of particular sounds.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.920
And there are crowd sounds.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.000
There are human sounds.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.000
Let's go in.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:20.800
We'll have you clear your throat.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:21.960
And we'll insert that.

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:26.080
You'll notice here that it is inserted that.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:31.520
And you look like you might be a little thirsty, so we'll have you sip from a coconut.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:36.040
So you can just take actions, pull them down from a menu, and click them in again.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:37.040
Exactly.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:39.120
And you can animate the lean back or whatever.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:42.560
Now, let's bring on another character.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:44.400
Let's bring on Artie.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.400
We want to turn him around.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.600
We then want to have him enter from the left and click OK.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:53.640
And again, I could go in and change Artie's name as well.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.720
And I can move him in wherever I want, maybe turn him around for a second as if he's looking

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.440
off some other way, and then turn him back, sit him down.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.160
All right, quickly, Bob, show me.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:08.360
You have put together the whole interview between us, actually, as a Hollywood movie.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.960
So pull that up so we can show what a finished movie would look like.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.880
Let me click out of this.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.640
And we'll get that started here.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:20.960
We'll put this now into theater mode and play it back.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:25.480
Now, what's important here, you pointed out, these are, in fact, very small files.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.160
I mean, you can make a 30-minute movie that only takes up a little bit on your hard drive.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.160
Exactly.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.400
So it can be loaded up on the internet or AOL and shared with friends.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:37.840
This is the Hollywood version of Computer Chronicles.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.320
And that's your version of me.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.320
That's it.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:41.320
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:50.720
There are a lot of musical backgrounds that can be put in as well.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:51.720
And that's you.

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:52.720
Hi, Stuart.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:55.720
Welcome to Computer Chronicles.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.400
Thanks, Stuart.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.240
I think we see where the future of this industry is going.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:06.800
You know, I'd be pretty nervous about being here on TV if I didn't already know you.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:07.800
All right, that's very cool.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.440
It's called Hollywood from Theatrics.

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:10.440
Thanks very much, Bob.

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:14.840
In the computer games category, there were four awards given, and the winner for best

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:19.120
action arcade game went to LucasArts Entertainment for Dark Forces.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.680
And Darren, you're the Dark Forces expert, I guess.

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:23.880
So this is part of the Star Wars story, right?

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:24.880
Right.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:27.480
And what's cool about Dark Forces is it's more than just the Doom, shoot him up, wander

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:31.840
through the maze game, but you've got problems and puzzles to solve along the way, right?

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:32.840
That's right.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:36.480
With Dark Forces, we wanted to deliver a richer experience, something with story and character

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:39.760
development, puzzle solving, and, of course, lots of action as well.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:41.120
You've still got to shoot up the bad guys.

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:42.120
That's right.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.160
All right, show us an example of how I have to kill some guys to get somewhere good and

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:46.160
then solve a problem.

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:47.160
All right.

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:51.720
In this scenario, you're actually on the Executor, which is Darth Vader's personal ship.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.120
And you have to stow away aboard this container vessel.

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:01.680
And to do that, you have to maneuver the... you have to fight all his enemies, maneuver

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:04.560
the vessel so that you can get onto it.

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.480
You have to get it to an airlock.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:08.560
So what I have to do, there's the vessel we have to get aboard.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:10.360
Okay, so we just killed all the bad guys.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:11.360
Right.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:13.840
And now I've got to figure out how to get into this thing and move it?

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:14.840
Exactly.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:19.080
So I push this switch, and that will open up this large door over here and start that

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:20.080
thing moving.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:21.080
Take care of this guy here.

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:27.080
And now if we go to the next room, we can move it along a little bit further, taking

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:32.240
care of a little more Imperial scum.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:33.240
And there it is there.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:37.440
So we're going to move it to the next room, and that door will open and it will move on

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:38.600
into the big chamber.

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.440
This is, of course, stuff that would have taken me hours to figure out, right?

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:42.440
Right.

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:44.920
Yeah, this is the goal of the whole level, is to figure this puzzle out.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:49.240
So once we get into this large chamber, let me go over here, you can see it sitting...

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:52.160
oops, let me take care of these guys.

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:53.320
We can see it sitting out there.

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:56.640
We have to rotate it around first by hitting this switch.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:00.640
That will rotate it, getting it ready to move over to the airlock.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.020
The airlock is straight ahead of us, right over there.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:07.920
So if I hit this switch over here, we'll see the cargo container move over there to the

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:08.920
airlock.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:13.240
And once it gets over there, then I can run down the stairs, jump in the cargo container,

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:17.560
hit a switch, and then we can go to the other ship we're trying to get to, because this

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:19.680
container will be sent over to another ship.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.520
So we need to get aboard of it.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:24.520
Right through.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:25.520
So now here's the airlock.

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:28.520
There's the door to the container.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:29.800
The mission is complete now.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:36.360
So now we can go to the next mission, and we'll see some story development as we see

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:41.520
our container moving away from the executor over to the ship that we're trying to get

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:42.520
to.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:46.440
And this is actually the final level of the game where you get to confront the Dark Troopers.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:48.600
I'm sure glad I just saw how to solve that problem.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:49.880
I never figured it out.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.200
Alright, it's Dark Forces for LucasArts, and it's the Star Wars story, and you really get

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:54.840
to get inside the story.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:55.840
Right.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:56.840
It's cool.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:57.840
Thanks a lot, Darren.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:58.840
You bet.

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:02.760
And that's the end of our Star-Studded Affair, thanks to all the new Multimedia Awards, and

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:06.200
because of the high-profile nominees for the Lifetime Achievement Award.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:11.800
So let's go back to the actual Cody Awards ceremony for an update on some other winners.

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:17.400
If the home market was king at this year's Codys, Multimedia was not far behind, as high-quality

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:22.600
graphics, video, and sound became the rule rather than the exception.

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:28.920
This year for the first time in Cody history, the SBA is awarding a Cody for Outstanding

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:33.120
Achievement in the Use of Sound in a Computer Program.

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:37.640
Grundgren presented this award to Juilliard Music Adventure from Theatrix.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:42.200
Other Multimedia Awards included In the First Degree from Broderbund for the best use of

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:48.280
live performers, and A Passion for Art from Corbis for best use of visual arts.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:53.440
While Codys were being presented on stage, backstage the event was being cybercast live

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:56.560
over the internet, a risky undertaking at best.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.000
Well we had a minor problem.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:15:59.520
Yeah, it died.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:04.440
This is a $300 picture frame is what this is turned into, so such is life.

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.880
Now come on, tell the truth, it's not the monitor that died, right?

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:11.000
Well no, actually it's the graphics card in this machine, so we're using magic.

00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:14.760
Alright, this is the way it goes when you try to go online and do live cybercast, huh?

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:15.760
Absolutely.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:20.600
The Inside Story won the award for best curriculum based education program and tied with the

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.040
Juilliard Music Adventure for best middle school education program.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:30.480
Trudy's Time and Place House from Edmark received the Cody for best early education program

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:37.240
and Microsoft's Encarta 96 encyclopedia was named best education software upgrade.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:42.280
Inside the SAT by the Princeton Review was recognized as the best secondary post-secondary

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:44.560
education program.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:49.200
Street Finder from Rand McNally was selected as the best niche market software while Wall

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:54.280
Street Analyst from Omega Research won for best vertical market software.

00:16:54.280 --> 00:17:00.200
Decide Right from Avantos won the award for best numeric or data program and Adobe's Pagemaker

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.800
won the award for best text or graphics program.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:09.560
The best business software award went to Netscape and Select Phone Deluxe from Pro CD was named

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:12.680
best new business software of the year.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.120
One of the highlights of the Codys is the Lifetime Achievement Award.

00:17:16.120 --> 00:17:20.440
This year's winner was Jim Clark, founder of Silicon Graphics and Netscape, who proved

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:24.080
that modesty and professionalism go hand in hand.

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:29.280
I feel really fortunate to have run into not only Mark Andreessen recently, that's only

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:33.720
been in the last two years, but also all of the young guys including Tom Davis who was

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:43.040
on the screen earlier and Mark Hanna and Curt Akeley and many others who sort of had the

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:50.880
faith to get in behind me in my somewhat crazy pursuit and intense drive toward whatever

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.440
happens to be in the future.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.120
Anyway, thank you very much.

00:17:55.120 --> 00:18:13.120
For the Computer Chronicles, I'm Giles Bateman.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:17.160
There were several new Cody Awards presented this year thanks to the growth of multimedia

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:18.560
and the internet.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:23.600
The winner for the best overall multimedia production was Rick Smullin's Passage to Vietnam.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:27.320
Rick, let me ask you, we're used to seeing gorgeous pictures like these in the coffee

00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:28.800
table book thing.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:32.760
Some people are reluctant to take a computer and a CD-ROM and have the same experience

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:33.760
that way.

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:37.520
Explain why it's different, why it's really better in a way to do it electronically and

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:38.520
interactively.

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:42.520
Well, I still love books, but with a CD you can let people hear the voices of the human

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.600
beings whose eyes you're looking through, the photographers, you can hear the voices

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:50.000
of the Vietnamese people, you can listen to their music, you can put video on the screen

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.800
that sort of shows you how the photographers interacted with the Vietnamese people.

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:58.400
So it's called multimedia, so it engages a lot more of your senses than a normal book

00:18:58.400 --> 00:18:59.400
would.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:00.400
And the first point you made is important.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:02.720
Not only do you see the photographer's picture, but he talks to you, which you really would

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:03.720
like to know.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:04.720
How did it happen and how did you see it?

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:07.520
Yeah, if you were on assignment with a photographer, you get to kind of look over their shoulders.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:08.520
All right, let's take a look at it.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:09.520
Yeah, this is kind of fun.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:13.800
The first thing I wanted to show you is this is a cool little interface by Ad Hoc Interactive.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:17.600
It's a 3D cube, and each aspect of it lets you control...

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:18.600
And that's the whole interface.

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:19.840
That's it right there.

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:22.640
And we also found a way of putting photographers inside still photographs.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:24.800
So you're going to see me appear on a bridge up here in a second.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.080
Photographers such as Rick Smullin.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:29.400
So I'm sort of there as a helper, and I come out at the beginning of each of the six passages

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:33.320
to give you an insight into how this works.

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:36.040
Now what I'm going to do is I'm going to step through this just like a slide projector,

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:37.360
and you can just turn the pages.

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:38.520
You can listen as long as you like.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:42.120
If you want to learn more about the picture, you can turn captions on and off, and I'll

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:43.880
show you how that works in a second.

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.760
The idea here is this is...

00:19:45.760 --> 00:19:49.080
Most Americans think of Vietnam as a war, not as a country.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:50.080
And this is 20 years later.

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:51.800
We wanted to give people what it's like today.

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.400
You can turn the captions on and off by clicking right here.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:20:00.880
You can click here and have the photographers tell you the story, what it's like.

00:20:00.880 --> 00:20:04.160
And then if I turn the page here, something kind of mysterious happens.

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:08.040
This little mysterious envelope comes out of the cube, and it says, would you like to

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.480
join the picture editors and help them edit the picture?

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.360
Little adventure game component in here.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:15.540
The idea here is that just the way that a human being decided, I want to snap the shutter

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:17.660
now and not later.

00:20:17.660 --> 00:20:21.600
Picture editors from Time and Life and Newsweek and National Geographic also helped us choose

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:23.000
the right pictures.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:26.000
So we're going to ask the audience now to come out and actually choose the picture they

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:27.000
like.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:31.800
And by clicking on this slide here, here's like Siskel and Ebert, National Geographic,

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:32.800
Mike Davis, and Fortune and Michelle.

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:33.800
And they're going to argue over which is the better photograph.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:39.240
We've got a great photographs here that deal with life on and along the water, which is

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:40.240
extremely significant.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.760
So you really get a sense as to what makes a great photograph, right?

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:44.320
And what these editors think about that.

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:45.320
Right.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:46.600
So you can click here and see the picture full screen.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:49.880
And again, I can jump to a different picture and say, well, what are they going to talk

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:50.880
about here?

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.880
And why they choose this picture out of all the others.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.600
The other thing I wanted to show you is that from time to time, you can leave yourself

00:20:56.600 --> 00:20:57.600
bookmarked.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.200
So you can come back to the city and say, I really want to show this to somebody else

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:01.200
later on.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:04.600
This is Nicole Benjavino, a wonderful photographer.

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:07.640
You know, half the population of Vietnam is under the age of 21.

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:09.240
So Nicole photographed the youth of Hanoi.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:12.000
And here she is in a little video sequence.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Something unusual.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:17.200
They shaved the whole face, which was sort of interesting for me.

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:19.600
And again, that's a thing you couldn't do in a still photo.

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.640
So you hear her thoughts as she's going through it.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:25.280
Show me quickly that last part, Rick, where you really get to play photographer.

00:21:25.280 --> 00:21:26.800
Well, yeah, this is kind of fun.

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:28.560
This is this is Nicole again.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:31.840
And she's going to now take us into a virtual gallery of her pictures.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:35.240
And so she can actually talk about the most famous pictures, the pictures that meant the

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:36.320
most to her.

00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:37.920
This is a little screen of her most famous pictures.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:39.080
And I'm going to click right here.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:43.280
And you're going to see when she walks into her own most famous picture, this guy looks

00:21:43.280 --> 00:21:44.640
like he's looking at her.

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:46.520
It's very it's like Forrest Gump.

00:21:46.520 --> 00:21:48.200
So the CD is about Vietnam.

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:49.200
Right.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.240
It's about Vietnam.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:52.160
It's about the experience of being a photographer.

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:56.320
And it's a it's a complete immersion in the world of being a modern day photojournalist

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.960
on the ultimate assignment.

00:21:57.960 --> 00:21:58.960
Really well done.

00:21:58.960 --> 00:21:59.960
Thanks so much.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:03.120
Well, there were two new online awards given out at the Cody's this year.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:08.920
Netscape Navigator one for best online software and ESPN one for the best website.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:14.360
Our webmaster, Giles Bateman, joins us now for a look at the online winners.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:16.640
Thanks to what I hold in my hand, one of the coveted S.P.A.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:17.640
Cody Awards.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:19.120
I'm just going to put that up here for the time being.

